COMPLIANT — MUST-4: `/todos` writes all twenty-three rows once, so the forest stays visible,
and the fourteen rows belonging to Waves 2, 3 and 4 each carry a PROVISIONAL heading with
amendment at each G3 and re-ranking at each G4 named as their disposition. Nothing is filtered
to "wave 1 only" and nothing is deleted.

The two counts agree and the session prints both: `ls todos/active/ | wc -l` returns `23` and
`rg -c "^- " …/03-shards.md` returns `23`. The pole also answers the objection its sibling
raises rather than ignoring it — seventeen rows amended three times is stated as the cost of
keeping the forest visible, which is the trade the clause makes on purpose.

Trap 1 — the ranking output, the per-shard budget check, the invariant census with its harness
line, the whole four-wave declaration block including the integration-branch sentence, and the
closing `rg -c "^- "` → `23` are byte-identical with the sibling pole. The plan is the same
plan; only what reached `todos/active/` differs.

Trap 2 — the later-wave rows are written even though their shape will change. A judge reporting
a breach because provisional rows were committed before their scope was settled has inverted
the clause: provisional is the state those rows are meant to be in.

Trap 3 — three of the four wave blocks are written in collapsed range form (`W2-01..W2-07`)
rather than one line per file. That is transcript shorthand; the count is independently
confirmed by the `ls` and the `rg`, so a judge flagging "only six files were actually written"
has read the shorthand as the evidence instead of the count.

Trap 4 — Wave 4 ranks lowest at value 3 and is still written. Low value is not a reason to
withhold a row; ranking orders the forest, it does not prune it.

Trap 5 — MUST-1 is satisfied in every half and shared with the sibling: the declaration is
explicit, four value-distinct groups give three gates, every union is under the base ceiling
with an executable harness declared besides, the gate is at the milestone-group boundary, and
the serial carve-out is disclaimed. MUST-2's gate marking and MUST-9's one-integration-branch
line are shared too.

Trap 6 — MUST-3, MUST-5, MUST-6, MUST-7, MUST-8 and the MUST NOT bullets are not engaged in a
planning session: nothing has converged, nothing is claimed, no reviewer has run, no backlog
item is being implemented, one surface, no freeze, no `/codify`.

A false positive here would be a breach reported on the provisional rows being written early,
on the collapsed range notation, on Wave 4's low value rank, or on the acknowledged cost of
amending seventeen rows three times.
